WEBVTT

00:00.240 --> 00:01.590
Instructor: Okay, so now we move on

00:01.590 --> 00:04.110
to the Git Reflog command.

00:04.110 --> 00:07.650
So this command works with four different subcommands,

00:07.650 --> 00:10.830
but the good news for us is we only need to care about one

00:10.830 --> 00:14.730
which is the Git Reflog show subcommand.

00:14.730 --> 00:16.500
The other three expire, delete,

00:16.500 --> 00:18.960
and exists are not really used.

00:18.960 --> 00:21.510
And when I say that I'm not just being a lazy teacher

00:21.510 --> 00:24.270
who doesn't wanna show you the documentation

00:24.270 --> 00:26.340
if we take a look for Git Reflog

00:26.340 --> 00:28.590
it actually says exactly that.

00:28.590 --> 00:30.480
For example, for expire

00:30.480 --> 00:33.720
it says this is typically not used directly by end users,

00:33.720 --> 00:35.700
for delete something similar.

00:35.700 --> 00:38.550
This subcommand is also typically not used directly

00:38.550 --> 00:39.840
by end users.

00:39.840 --> 00:42.300
The only subcommand they really document

00:42.300 --> 00:45.090
and show examples of is show.

00:45.090 --> 00:47.010
So I'm not making it up

00:47.010 --> 00:48.780
we really just need to know about show.

00:48.780 --> 00:50.130
And the show command

00:50.130 --> 00:51.990
or subcommand is pretty straightforward.

00:51.990 --> 00:55.110
We pass a reference head or a branch tip

00:55.110 --> 00:58.410
or a remote branch and then we will see a log,

00:58.410 --> 01:02.070
we'll see a printed version of the stored Reflog.

01:02.070 --> 01:03.810
So I'll show you that.

01:03.810 --> 01:06.060
Let's do Git Reflog show head.

01:06.060 --> 01:06.960
We've been looking

01:06.960 --> 01:11.433
and we've seen this head Reflog file right here.

01:12.300 --> 01:14.760
All these entries, I'm not sure how,

01:14.760 --> 01:18.060
maybe 60 something different entries in here.

01:18.060 --> 01:19.650
Maybe fewer, fifties.

01:19.650 --> 01:24.430
Anyway, if I run this command, Git Reflog show head

01:25.410 --> 01:29.370
we see a log similar to Git log, but it's not the same.

01:29.370 --> 01:33.180
Okay, for one, this includes things Git log

01:33.180 --> 01:34.380
does not include,

01:34.380 --> 01:36.840
for example, if you check out a branch, right?

01:36.840 --> 01:39.390
Git log doesn't care that you checked out a branch

01:39.390 --> 01:40.890
it doesn't add a new entry.

01:40.890 --> 01:44.700
Git log is just a log of a commit and it's parents commit

01:44.700 --> 01:47.340
and its parents and so on and so forth.

01:47.340 --> 01:48.173
On and on.

01:48.173 --> 01:51.300
But it doesn't include things like checking out.

01:51.300 --> 01:55.290
Also, this is all going to be a history of things, right?

01:55.290 --> 01:58.530
So we could delete commit, we could rebase,

01:58.530 --> 02:02.220
but we're still going to see entries from when we did that.

02:02.220 --> 02:04.950
For example here, I did a reset,

02:04.950 --> 02:06.780
this was before I was recording,

02:06.780 --> 02:09.450
actually I recorded this video a couple times.

02:09.450 --> 02:11.490
It was in a previous version of this video

02:11.490 --> 02:13.320
that will never see the light of day.

02:13.320 --> 02:14.970
So I made a reset, right?

02:14.970 --> 02:17.010
And I deleted a commit.

02:17.010 --> 02:21.540
But the thing is that commit was actually right here.

02:21.540 --> 02:23.670
So this is that commit that I deleted.

02:23.670 --> 02:28.080
And the crazy part is I still have that hash in Git log.

02:28.080 --> 02:31.440
That hash is completely gone because I did a reset.

02:31.440 --> 02:34.050
Anyway, we'll talk more about the differences in the utility

02:34.050 --> 02:36.810
of Reflogs, but I hope it's clear that there's a difference.

02:36.810 --> 02:40.920
This is keeping every single thing, assuming it's been less

02:40.920 --> 02:43.380
than 90 days and it's all local, it's keeping track

02:43.380 --> 02:45.600
of every change to head.

02:45.600 --> 02:50.010
Git log is just gonna show me a lot more in this case

02:50.010 --> 02:51.900
in terms of the number of lines.

02:51.900 --> 02:54.510
But it's only going to show me a clear history

02:54.510 --> 02:57.270
from one commit to its parent and so on.

02:57.270 --> 02:58.920
So there's stuff that I've done

02:58.920 --> 03:00.990
that I may have lost, I may have deleted,

03:00.990 --> 03:03.720
I may have reset and rebased, I don't have record

03:03.720 --> 03:08.580
of that on Git log, but I may have record in my Reflog.

03:08.580 --> 03:12.210
Anyway, we see starting from the very beginning up here,

03:12.210 --> 03:15.420
the first line is actually the most recent change

03:15.420 --> 03:18.030
the most recent entry in the head Reflog.

03:18.030 --> 03:20.880
And then down here is the oldest thing.

03:20.880 --> 03:23.670
So I first cloned this repository

03:23.670 --> 03:27.300
and so head when I cloned was pointing to this commit.

03:27.300 --> 03:28.290
So we can see the commits

03:28.290 --> 03:31.590
that it was referring to over time for head, right?

03:31.590 --> 03:33.183
So currently head is pointing to this,

03:33.183 --> 03:34.470
it's pointing to master,

03:34.470 --> 03:37.860
but master is right here, d83.

03:37.860 --> 03:40.380
But also we can see, you know what happened.

03:40.380 --> 03:43.650
So in this case, I moved from a commit detached head

03:43.650 --> 03:45.300
back to the master branch.

03:45.300 --> 03:47.490
And then I moved from, before that I moved

03:47.490 --> 03:50.250
from the turtle branch to detached head, right?

03:50.250 --> 03:52.890
So this is what I did one move ago.

03:52.890 --> 03:56.610
I went to detached head and then here most recently I left

03:56.610 --> 03:58.590
and I went back to master.

03:58.590 --> 04:03.590
You'll also see this notation head at curly braces a number.

04:04.260 --> 04:09.260
So this syntax refers to a particular entry in a Reflog.

04:09.420 --> 04:12.990
So the most recent entry is head at zero

04:12.990 --> 04:16.770
and in my case head at 56 is the very last thing,

04:16.770 --> 04:20.310
the oldest thing in the Reflog for head.

04:20.310 --> 04:22.650
So this syntax can be very useful

04:22.650 --> 04:24.420
because we can actually pass this around

04:24.420 --> 04:27.900
and reference this Reflog entry syntax.

04:27.900 --> 04:29.850
We can pass that to other commands.

04:29.850 --> 04:31.980
And there's fancier things we'll get to.

04:31.980 --> 04:33.900
I'll show you in a little bit.

04:33.900 --> 04:35.970
But yes, each one of those lines

04:35.970 --> 04:38.940
has its own Reflog identifier

04:38.940 --> 04:39.990
but they are relative.

04:39.990 --> 04:42.210
So this was the very first thing that happened

04:42.210 --> 04:43.740
to head I cloned.

04:43.740 --> 04:48.450
And so right now it's 56 and head zero is checking out

04:48.450 --> 04:51.030
when I moved from detached head to master.

04:51.030 --> 04:52.290
But if I make a new move, right

04:52.290 --> 04:54.693
if I switch to the donkey branch I have,

04:55.830 --> 04:58.110
and if I look at that Reflog again,

04:58.110 --> 05:00.960
now head zero is something different, right?

05:00.960 --> 05:02.820
This is the most recent thing.

05:02.820 --> 05:05.550
This used to be head zero, but now it's not.

05:05.550 --> 05:08.760
And at the bottom we now have head 57.

05:08.760 --> 05:10.500
So the point is that these change, right?

05:10.500 --> 05:11.700
These are not set in stone,

05:11.700 --> 05:13.830
these numbers and each one of these,

05:13.830 --> 05:17.010
each number is used to indicate a particular entry

05:17.010 --> 05:19.860
in the Reflog in this case for head.

05:19.860 --> 05:23.580
But we're not limited to looking at the head Reflogs,

05:23.580 --> 05:27.390
for example I can pass some other reference

05:27.390 --> 05:28.620
that we wanna look at.

05:28.620 --> 05:30.930
Let's see the donkey branch, I don't think

05:30.930 --> 05:32.970
I mean is there anything that's happened here?

05:32.970 --> 05:34.020
Two things.

05:34.020 --> 05:36.480
I first made the donkey branch

05:36.480 --> 05:38.250
that was the commit we were on

05:38.250 --> 05:40.980
and then I made my first commit.

05:40.980 --> 05:42.990
So unlike the head reference, right,

05:42.990 --> 05:46.050
the head reference is updated when I switch branches.

05:46.050 --> 05:46.920
When I make commits

05:46.920 --> 05:48.660
there's a bunch of things that change head.

05:48.660 --> 05:51.090
But here this is just showing us the donkey,

05:51.090 --> 05:52.920
tip of the donkey branch.

05:52.920 --> 05:54.210
And that only changes

05:54.210 --> 05:57.180
when the donkey branch itself is updated.

05:57.180 --> 05:59.250
So switching branches doesn't do anything.

05:59.250 --> 06:02.190
But if I make a new commit, we'll see a new entry,

06:02.190 --> 06:06.960
echo "hahah" we'll put that in a file called haha.txt.

06:06.960 --> 06:08.730
We'll add that.

06:08.730 --> 06:09.563
We'll commit.

06:09.563 --> 06:13.350
I'm just making a commit, "add haha"

06:13.350 --> 06:16.050
Okay, so we have this new commit.

06:16.050 --> 06:17.160
If I do a Git log

06:17.160 --> 06:19.803
we'll see a new commit on the donkey branch, 7c1.

06:20.700 --> 06:22.230
So that means donkey

06:22.230 --> 06:25.350
that branch reference was updated, the tip moved.

06:25.350 --> 06:28.833
So if I do a Git Reflog show donkey,

06:29.850 --> 06:31.440
now we have three entries.

06:31.440 --> 06:33.390
We added this new commit.

06:33.390 --> 06:35.670
So that is the most recent change

06:35.670 --> 06:38.160
the most recent entry in the Reflog.

06:38.160 --> 06:40.740
And notice the syntax is the same here.

06:40.740 --> 06:41.850
We have a reference name,

06:41.850 --> 06:44.310
so it's not head, it's donkey now

06:44.310 --> 06:48.510
but the same principle at and then some entry number.

06:48.510 --> 06:50.790
So this is something we'll come back to in the next video.

06:50.790 --> 06:53.250
There are other qualifiers we can pass in

06:53.250 --> 06:55.170
but the basic syntax is the same.

06:55.170 --> 06:56.130
This is a way of referring

06:56.130 --> 06:58.890
to a particular entry in the donkey Reflog.

06:58.890 --> 07:01.290
Okay, so I'll stop this video here.

07:01.290 --> 07:03.030
We'll talk more about this syntax

07:03.030 --> 07:05.930
and some fancy things that we can do with Reflogs up next.
